-- Passage rg7-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6ac60994-f493-539a-acd1-bc3548aa98b9', 'rg7-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Canal That Built a Town$q$, $q$In the early 1800s, the village of Harlow Falls was home to fewer than two hundred people. Farmers there grew plenty of wheat, but they had trouble selling it. The nearest large city was eighty miles away, and the roads were rough dirt paths. A wagon trip could take more than a week, and much of the profit went to paying the drivers.

Then the state decided to dig a canal that would pass right by the village. Work began in 1821 and lasted six years. When the canal opened, a single horse could pull a loaded boat that carried as much as twenty wagons. Shipping costs dropped sharply.

The change came quickly. Farmers who once grew only enough for their own families began planting larger fields, because they could now sell their extra grain in the city. Mills went up along the canal to grind the wheat into flour before shipping it. The mills needed workers, so new families arrived. Shops, a bank, and a school soon followed.

By 1840, Harlow Falls had more than three thousand residents. The canal had also brought ideas. Boats carried newspapers, books, and travelers with news from distant places, and the village started its first library.

The good times did not last forever. In the 1850s, a railroad was built through a town twelve miles north. Trains were faster than canal boats, and they could run all winter, when the canal froze. Slowly, business moved to the railroad town, and Harlow Falls stopped growing. Today, visitors can still walk along the old towpath and see the stone walls of the mills that the canal once made possible.$q$, 'draft')
