-- Passage rg7-connections-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f64504c5-9200-5e07-854d-da0754dd1b31', 'rg7-connections-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Smaller Fields, Bigger Skills$q$, $q$For years, the youngest players in the Ridgeview Youth Soccer League played on full-sized fields with eleven players on each team. Coaches noticed a problem. In a typical game, many children touched the ball only a few times. The fastest kids chased it while the others stood and watched.

In 2019, league director Marisol Tanaka proposed a change. Players under ten would play seven against seven on fields about half the usual size. Some parents worried that the small games would not prepare children for real soccer. Tanaka asked the league to try the plan for one season and count what happened.

Volunteers recorded every game. On the smaller fields, each player touched the ball about three times as often as before. Because the field was shorter, players had to make quick decisions and pass in tight spaces. Goalkeepers faced more shots, so they improved faster too.

The numbers surprised some of the parents who had doubted the plan. Coaches also reported that fewer children quit the league during the season. Several players said the games felt more fun because they were always involved.

The league kept the new format. Tanaka explained that the goal was not to make soccer easier. It was to give every child more chances to practice the skills the full game requires. When those players moved up to larger fields at age eleven, coaches said they handled the ball with more confidence than earlier groups had.$q$, 'draft')
