-- Passage rg7-connections-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2547e711-2af7-5ca5-a639-9b92afb53141', 'rg7-connections-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Library That Lends Tools$q$, $q$In the town of Brookhaven, a small building next to the public library holds something unusual. Its shelves are lined with drills, ladders, garden rakes, and sewing machines. Anyone with a library card can borrow them, just like books.

The idea started with a problem that librarian Priya Castellanos kept hearing about. Residents told her they needed a tool for one small job, such as hanging a shelf, but did not want to buy one they would rarely use. Meanwhile, other residents had garages full of tools that sat unused for years.

Castellanos saw that the two problems could solve each other. She asked people to donate tools they no longer needed. Within a month, the library had collected more than four hundred items. Volunteers who were skilled at repairs offered to check each tool and fix the broken ones.

The tool library changed how some residents spent their weekends. Families who had never tried home projects began building garden boxes and fixing bicycles. Because borrowers often had questions, the volunteers started teaching free Saturday classes. Those classes became so popular that the library added a waiting list.

The project has also saved money. A neighbor who borrows a pressure washer for one afternoon avoids spending a large amount on a machine that would sit in a closet. Less waste ends up in the town's landfill, since fewer people buy tools they will throw away later.

Castellanos says the biggest change is harder to measure. People who once only nodded to each other now trade advice about paint colors and leaky faucets.$q$, 'draft')
