-- Passage rg7-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('424826ed-e0dd-5dfd-b1b5-6d447c8dfab4', 'rg7-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Growing a Reef Back$q$, $q$When marine biologist Tomas Achebe first dove at Sandal Reef in 2008, he saw wide stretches of white, bleached coral. Warmer water had stressed the coral, causing it to lose the tiny algae that give it food and color. Many of the coral colonies had died.

Achebe knew that some coral can regrow from small broken pieces. He wondered whether people could help this process along. With a small team, he built underwater frames shaped like trees out of plastic pipe. The team hung pieces of living coral from the branches, where they could grow without being buried by sand or eaten by fish.

At first, the experiment struggled. A storm knocked over two frames, and many pieces died. Achebe and his team redesigned the frames with heavier bases and moved them to a calmer spot. The next year, most of the coral pieces survived and doubled in size.

The results drew attention from people who lived along the coast. Local dive shops began offering trips where visitors could help clean the frames. Fishing families, who depended on healthy reefs, volunteered to watch the nursery for damage. A nearby school started a class in which students measured the coral's growth each month.

Once the coral pieces grew large enough, divers attached them to the damaged reef. Over the following years, fish returned to some of the replanted areas. Achebe has pointed out that the nursery cannot fix the warming water that caused the damage. Still, the project showed that a single idea, shared with a whole community, could give a reef a better chance to recover.$q$, 'draft')
