-- Passage rg7-connections-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('78b076f0-b753-57f4-9d82-9f13f2df6597', 'rg7-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why the Late Bus Matters$q$, $q$Two years ago, our school added a late bus that leaves at 4:45 in the afternoon, more than an hour after the regular buses. Now the district is considering cutting it to save money. I believe that would be a mistake, because the late bus has changed far more than the bus schedule.

Before the late bus existed, students who lived far from school had a hard choice. If they stayed for a club or tutoring, they needed a ride home from a family member. Many families work until evening, so those students simply went home on the regular bus. As a result, clubs were filled mostly with students who lived close by or had a parent available to drive.

The late bus removed that barrier. In its first year, the robotics club grew from nine members to twenty-three. The after-school tutoring room, which used to be half empty, now has a sign-up sheet. Teachers have told our student council that math grades improved among students who attend tutoring regularly.

There is another effect that is easy to miss. Because students from across the district now join clubs, they make friends outside their own neighborhoods. Our robotics team includes students from five different elementary schools, and they learned to work together under pressure at their first competition.

I understand that the district has to make hard choices about money. However, cutting the late bus would undo all of these gains at once. The clubs would shrink again, and the students who need tutoring the most would lose access to it. Instead, the district could look for savings elsewhere or ask local businesses to sponsor the route.$q$, 'draft')
